1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,048
How do you make sure a card is charged exactly once?

2
00:00:03,098 --> 00:00:09,432
In Lesson 26, the page took a token from the card frame, and ecom checked it belongs to this payment.

3
00:00:09,482 --> 00:00:14,088
The fee from Lesson 22 and the terminal from Lesson 23 are ready too.

4
00:00:14,138 --> 00:00:17,448
This lesson charges the card, exactly once.

5
00:00:17,498 --> 00:00:19,872
Picture the buyer pressing pay twice.

6
00:00:19,922 --> 00:00:23,208
Or the page retrying on a weak network.

7
00:00:23,258 --> 00:00:27,408
Or two copies of the bank's app picking up the same confirmed card.

8
00:00:27,458 --> 00:00:29,280
Each of them could charge the card.

9
00:00:29,330 --> 00:00:31,176
Now picture a worse case.

10
00:00:31,226 --> 00:00:35,904
The app asks the network to charge the card, and crashes before it saves the answer.

11
00:00:35,954 --> 00:00:37,776
Did money move?

12
00:00:37,826 --> 00:00:39,648
Nobody knows.

13
00:00:39,698 --> 00:00:41,520
Think of a bank's cash desk.

14
00:00:41,570 --> 00:00:44,376
Only one cashier can hold the withdrawal slip.

15
00:00:44,426 --> 00:00:49,056
And the cashier writes the withdrawal in the logbook before opening the drawer.

16
00:00:49,106 --> 00:00:50,928
The charge works the same way.

17
00:00:50,978 --> 00:00:52,800
First comes the claim.

18
00:00:52,850 --> 00:00:57,384
The claim is a single change to the payment, from card confirmed to debiting.

19
00:00:57,434 --> 00:01:00,888
It succeeds only if the payment is still card confirmed.

20
00:01:00,938 --> 00:01:05,976
So however many clicks or retries arrive, exactly one caller wins.

21
00:01:06,026 --> 00:01:09,144
The others find the payment already taken, and stop.

22
00:01:09,194 --> 00:01:11,448
The winner then finds two things.

23
00:01:11,498 --> 00:01:14,424
It finds the website's live terminal for this card.

24
00:01:14,474 --> 00:01:17,640
And it finds the fee the bank agreed with this merchant.

25
00:01:17,690 --> 00:01:22,248
If either is missing, the payment fails right there, and nothing is charged.

26
00:01:22,298 --> 00:01:25,776
Next, the winner writes a line in the operation journal.

27
00:01:25,826 --> 00:01:29,112
That is the bank's logbook of every call that moves money.

28
00:01:29,162 --> 00:01:34,440
The line says debit, requested, for this payment, through this terminal.

29
00:01:34,490 --> 00:01:37,320
It is written before any network is asked.

30
00:01:37,370 --> 00:01:40,248
And a payment can hold only one debit line.

31
00:01:40,298 --> 00:01:43,776
Then ecom asks the card zone to charge the card by its token.

32
00:01:43,826 --> 00:01:45,648
The call carries its own id.

33
00:01:45,698 --> 00:01:51,816
If the call is sent twice, the card zone sees the same id, and the charge still happens once.

34
00:01:51,866 --> 00:01:56,040
The card zone talks to the processing centre, which asks the buyer's bank.

35
00:01:56,090 --> 00:01:58,704
Some networks only hold the money at first.

36
00:01:58,754 --> 00:02:02,472
For those, the card zone takes the held money at once.

37
00:02:02,522 --> 00:02:04,632
That second step is the capture.

38
00:02:04,682 --> 00:02:08,808
So for every network, approved means the money is taken.

39
00:02:08,858 --> 00:02:10,680
Three answers can come back.

40
00:02:10,730 --> 00:02:13,632
Approved means paid, with a reference number.

41
00:02:13,682 --> 00:02:17,304
Declined is final, with a reason the bank can explain.

42
00:02:17,354 --> 00:02:19,800
And no answer in time is unknown.

43
00:02:19,850 --> 00:02:22,224
Unknown is never treated as declined.

44
00:02:22,274 --> 00:02:25,248
A paid payment never becomes unpaid again.

45
00:02:25,298 --> 00:02:27,120
Now watch it from the buyer's side.

46
00:02:27,170 --> 00:02:30,600
The buyer types the card into the frame and confirms it there.

47
00:02:30,650 --> 00:02:35,472
The page changes to taking the payment, and says the buyer's bank is being asked.

48
00:02:35,522 --> 00:02:39,768
A few seconds later, it says paid, and shows the reference number.

49
00:02:39,818 --> 00:02:41,640
Try a card with too little money.

50
00:02:41,690 --> 00:02:47,352
The page says the payment was declined, and gives the reason: there isn't enough money on the card.

51
00:02:47,402 --> 00:02:49,224
Now open the merchant portal.

52
00:02:49,274 --> 00:02:52,056
The payments list shows the payment as paid.

53
00:02:52,106 --> 00:02:52,872
Open it.

54
00:02:52,922 --> 00:02:56,448
The details show the amount, the website and the bank fee.

55
00:02:56,498 --> 00:02:59,328
Below them is a section called calls to the card scheme.

56
00:02:59,378 --> 00:03:03,048
It has one line: debit, first sent, now approved.

57
00:03:03,098 --> 00:03:06,408
Even after a double click, there is still only one line.

58
00:03:06,458 --> 00:03:08,280
What does this protect?

59
00:03:08,330 --> 00:03:11,736
Without the claim, a double click is a double charge.

60
00:03:11,786 --> 00:03:17,280
Without the journal written first, a crash leaves money moved and no record of it.

61
00:03:17,330 --> 00:03:21,984
Without the once-only id, a retried call charges the card again.

62
00:03:22,034 --> 00:03:29,472
And without the unknown outcome, a slow network would tell the buyer to pay again, while the first charge quietly went through.

63
00:03:29,522 --> 00:03:31,368
The buyer is charged once.

64
00:03:31,418 --> 00:03:33,384
The merchant is paid once.

65
00:03:33,434 --> 00:03:36,240
And the bank can always say what it asked, and when.

66
00:03:36,290 --> 00:03:38,112
So back to the cash desk.

67
00:03:38,162 --> 00:03:41,688
One slip, in one hand, written in the logbook first.

68
00:03:41,738 --> 00:03:43,560
One charge.

69
00:03:43,610 --> 00:03:46,656
Next: what happens when the network goes silent.
